<h2>Start from the disclosure regime</h2>
<p>Modern maintenance practice is affidavit-driven. Both sides must file sworn statements of assets and liabilities covering income from every source, movable and immovable property, bank accounts and investments, liabilities, dependants and monthly expenditure. The affidavit serves two purposes: it gives the court a baseline for interim maintenance without waiting for trial, and it commits each party to a verifiable account which cross-examination and summoned documents can later demolish. For the applicant, the single most valuable step at the interim stage is a pointed, paragraph-wise rebuttal of the respondent&rsquo;s affidavit, marrying each denial to a document.</p>

<h2>The documentary net for business income</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it shows</th><th>How it is obtained</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Income tax returns &amp; computation (3-5 years)</td><td>Declared income, depreciation claims, business turnover trends</td><td>Direction to file; summons to the department where refused</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GST returns</td><td>Actual turnover of the business, often far above the ITR picture</td><td>Direction to the party; third-party summons</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bank and credit card statements</td><td>Cash flow, EMIs serviced, lifestyle spending, transfers to family</td><td>Direction; summons to banks where accounts are concealed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Balance sheets / partnership deeds / MCA filings</td><td>Capital, partner\'s share, related-party structures</td><td>Public records; summons to the firm or company</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Property records &amp; rent agreements</td><td>Rental income, ownership hidden behind relatives</td><td>Sub-registrar records; municipal records; summons to tenants</td></tr>
    <tr><td>School fee, club, travel and insurance records</td><td>Standard of living actually maintained</td><td>Receipts with the applicant; summons where needed</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Lifestyle as evidence</h2>
<p>Where documents are thin — the genuinely cash-based trade — courts fall back on the standard-of-living principle: the applicant is entitled to live, as nearly as may be, at the standard the parties enjoyed together, and the respondent&rsquo;s visible expenditure is evidence of capacity. Photographs of the matrimonial home, the cars maintained, destination events, private school admissions and foreign holidays all become relevant. The respondent who pleads penury while servicing two car loans should expect the plea to be weighed accordingly.</p>

<h2>Adverse inference: the court\'s real leverage</h2>
<p>The disclosure regime works because concealment carries a price. Where a party refuses disclosure, files a demonstrably false affidavit, or suppresses accounts later unearthed, the court may draw an adverse inference and fix maintenance on an attributed income — a figure the court considers the respondent must reasonably be earning given his qualifications, business, assets and lifestyle. In practice this is how most self-employed cases are decided: not by finding the true figure to the rupee, but by attributing a defensible figure the concealing party has forfeited the right to contest in detail.</p>

<h2>Both directions: the applicant\'s capacity too</h2>
<p>The same tools cut both ways. The applicant&rsquo;s qualifications, past employment and independent income are equally in issue: maintenance is a protection against destitution and an equalisation of standards, not a pension for a qualified professional who chooses not to work. An applicant with genuine earning history should expect it to be weighed; an applicant who gave up work for caregiving should plead and prove that history, because it explains the gap the respondent will otherwise exploit.</p>

<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>File the rebuttal affidavit early</strong> — interim maintenance is fixed on the affidavits; a bare denial wastes the stage that matters most.</li>
  <li><strong>Summon third-party records</strong> — banks, the GST portal, the school, the RWA — rather than arguing about what the respondent will not produce.</li>
  <li><strong>Plead specific lifestyle facts</strong> with dates and documents; adjectives achieve nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep your own affidavit scrupulously accurate</strong> — adverse inference is symmetrical, and credibility once lost infects the whole case.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Maintenance orders are variable, not final. Where income is proved to have risen — a business that flourished after the interim order — an application for enhancement lies; where it genuinely falls, reduction can be sought. The disclosure toolkit above serves both applications, and the party with the cleaner record ordinarily wins the variation round as well.</p>
</div>
